---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="NO BALL GAMES Lore | Characters, Signs, Streets & Restricted Zones"
  description="Enter the NO BALL GAMES lore archive: signs, streets, characters, restricted zones, secret games and the mythology behind the brand."
  current="lore"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">Lore archive</span>
    <h1>The sign was only the first warning.</h1>
    <p>
      NO BALL GAMES can work as a full story world. The banned court, the blocked estate, the painted wall and the secret game all become part of the mythology.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="timeline">
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Origin</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>The sign</h3>
          <p>A public warning designed to stop play becomes the central symbol of the brand.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Phase one</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>The wall</h3>
          <p>The message moves from sign to surface: tags, grime texture, characters, posters, stencils and coded marks.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Phase two</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>The players</h3>
          <p>Characters emerge from the restricted zones. Each one carries a different version of rebellion, humour, survival or creative defiance.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Phase three</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>The movement</h3>
          <p>The brand becomes a hub for collectors, artists, gamers, writers and supporters who understand the meaning behind the phrase.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section split-grid">
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">World ingredients</span>
      <h2>Simple objects. Bigger meaning.</h2>
      <p>
        The lore should stay grounded in familiar British visual language: signs, fences, courts, alleyways, estate blocks, shutters, bus stops, school walls and concrete spaces.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">Useful tags</span>
      <div class="tag-cloud">
        <span>restricted zones</span><span>secret games</span><span>estate mythology</span><span>graffiti codes</span><span>street characters</span><span>blocked courts</span><span>urban folklore</span><span>collector lore</span>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
